@node GOAL Language Forms
@section GOAL Language Forms

GOAL is a Lisp-like functional language designed for game scripting and system programming
on 8-bit and 16-bit platforms. This section documents the 10+ primary forms that constitute
the language, with complete syntax, numeric type information, real examples, and domain features.

@subsection GOAL Form: DEFUN (Function Definition)

@cindex DEFUN form in GOAL
@cindex function definition

The @code{DEFUN} form defines a named function with parameters and a body. It is the primary
mechanism for code organization and reuse in GOAL.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
(DEFUN function-name (param1 param2 ... paramN) body-expression)
@end example

@strong{Parameters}:
@itemize
@item @code{function-name}: Kebab-case identifier naming the function
@item @code{param-list}: Zero or more parameters, normalized to lowercase
@item @code{body-expression}: Single expression evaluated when function is called
@end itemize

@strong{Numeric Types Supported}:
@itemize
@item @code{BINARY}: Base-2 literals (0b1010, 0b11110000)
@item @code{DECIMAL}: Base-10 literals (42, 255, 1000)
@item @code{HEXADECIMAL}: Base-16 literals with #x prefix (#xFF, #xDEADBEEF)
@item @code{OCTAL}: Base-8 literals with #o prefix (#o77, #o755)
@end itemize

@strong{Example 1: Simple Arithmetic Function}:
@example
(DEFUN calculate-health (base damage)
  (- base damage))

; Usage: (calculate-health 100 25) => 75
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Using Different Numeric Types}:
@example
(DEFUN process-game-state (flags health-decimal health-hex)
  (let ((flag-binary #b1010)
        (max-health-oct #o377))
    (if (> health-decimal flags)
        (+ health-hex max-health-oct)
        (- health-decimal flag-binary))))

; Usage: (process-game-state #b1111 100 #xFF) => calculated value
@end example

@strong{Example 3: Recursive Function with BINARY Type}:
@example
(DEFUN bit-count (value count)
  (if (= value 0)
      count
      (bit-count (>> value 1) (+ count (& value #b1)))))

; Counts set bits in binary representation
; Usage: (bit-count #b11010011 0) => 5
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Multi-parameter Function Combining Types}:
@example
(DEFUN player-damage-calculation (base-damage #xFF weapon-bonus
                                   elemental-modifier)
  (let ((decimal-base (+ base-damage weapon-bonus))
        (hex-max #xFF))
    (if (> decimal-base hex-max)
        hex-max
        (+ decimal-base elemental-modifier))))

; Usage: (player-damage-calculation 50 #xFF 15 10) => 75
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Factorial with HEXADECIMAL Type}:
@example
(DEFUN factorial (n accumulator)
  (if (<= n #x1)
      accumulator
      (factorial (- n 1) (* n accumulator))))

; Usage: (factorial #x5 1) => 120
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
Functions in GOAL support tail-call optimization for efficient recursion, critical on
memory-constrained 8-bit systems. All numeric types are transparently converted during
compilation to the target CPU's native representation. Function definitions can reference
each other and form mutual recursion patterns.

---

@subsection GOAL Form: LET (Local Binding)

@cindex LET form in GOAL
@cindex local variable binding

The @code{LET} form creates local variable bindings within a scope, with all variables
initialized in parallel before any expressions in the body execute.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
(LET ((var1 init1) (var2 init2) ... (varN initN))
  body-expression)
@end example

@strong{Parameters}:
@itemize
@item @code{bindings}: List of (variable initial-value) pairs
@item @code{body-expression}: Expression evaluated with bindings in scope
@end itemize

@strong{Numeric Types Supported}:
All four numeric types (BINARY, DECIMAL, HEXADECIMAL, OCTAL) can be used in initializers.

@strong{Example 1: Simple Local Variables}:
@example
(let ((player-health 100)
      (enemy-health 50)
      (damage 15))
  (- player-health damage))

; Result: 85
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Mixed Numeric Types}:
@example
(let ((damage-decimal 25)
      (max-damage-hex #xFF)
      (min-damage-binary #b0001)
      (difficulty-octal #o3))
  (+ damage-decimal difficulty-octal))

; Result: 28
@end example

@strong{Example 3: Using let in Function Context}:
@example
(defun calculate-experience (monster-level base-xp multiplier)
  (let ((level-bonus (* monster-level #x10))
        (total-decimal (+ base-xp 50)))
    (- (* total-decimal multiplier) level-bonus)))

; Usage: (calculate-experience 5 100 2) => complex value
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Nested let Expressions}:
@example
(defun nested-calc (a b)
  (let ((x (+ a 10)))
    (let ((y (* x b)))
      (+ x y))))

; Usage: (nested-calc 5 2) => 40
@end example

@strong{Example 5: let with Conditional**:
@example
(let ((current-hp 75)
      (max-hp 100)
      (heal-amount 30))
  (if (< (+ current-hp heal-amount) max-hp)
      (+ current-hp heal-amount)
      max-hp))

; Result: 100
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
LET bindings are evaluated in parallel (non-sequentially), so each initializer cannot
reference variables bound earlier in the same let. For sequential binding, use LET*.
This parallel evaluation allows GOAL to generate efficient code on parallel architectures.

---

@subsection GOAL Form: IF (Conditional)

@cindex IF form in GOAL
@cindex conditional expression

The @code{IF} form provides conditional evaluation: if a test expression is true,
the then-expression is evaluated; otherwise, the else-expression is evaluated.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
(IF test-expr then-expr [else-expr])
@end example

@strong{Parameters}:
@itemize
@item @code{test-expr}: Expression evaluated for truthiness
@item @code{then-expr}: Evaluated when test is true
@item @code{else-expr}: Optional; evaluated when test is false
@end itemize

@strong{Numeric Types Supported}:
Test expressions can compare values of all four numeric types.

@strong{Example 1: Simple Conditional}:
@example
(if (> player-health 0)
    "alive"
    "dead")
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Conditional with Multiple Types**:
@example
(if (< current-damage #xFF)
    (+ current-damage #b1010)
    (- current-damage #o10))

; Compares decimal with hexadecimal
@end example

@strong{Example 3: Nested if**:
@example
(if (> level 5)
    (if (< experience 1000)
        "level-up"
        "ready-for-next")
    "low-level")
@end example

@strong{Example 4: if in Function**:
@example
(defun grade-score (score)
  (if (>= score 90)
      "A"
      (if (>= score 80)
          "B"
          (if (>= score 70)
              "C"
              "F"))))
@end example

@strong{Example 5: if with Complex Expressions**:
@example
(if (and (> health 50)
         (< mana 100)
         (= status :active))
    (cast-spell 'fireball)
    (cast-spell 'heal))
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
IF expressions compile to highly optimized conditional branches on target CPUs. The else
clause is optional; if omitted and test is false, IF returns NIL. IF supports both
arithmetic and logical tests, with automatic type promotion across numeric formats.

---

@subsection GOAL Form: COND (Multi-Branch Conditional)

@cindex COND form in GOAL
@cindex multi-branch conditional

The @code{COND} form provides a clean syntax for multiple mutually exclusive branches,
similar to a switch statement but with condition expressions rather than constant values.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
(COND (test1 expr1)
      (test2 expr2)
      ...
      (testN exprN)
      (T default-expr))
@end example

@strong{Parameters}:
@itemize
@item @code{test-expr}: Condition to evaluate
@item @code{expr}: Expression evaluated if test is true
@item @code{T}: Literal symbol representing the default case
@end itemize

@strong{Numeric Types Supported}:
Each test can use any combination of numeric types.

@strong{Example 1: Simple COND**:
@example
(cond ((< health 30) "critical")
      ((< health 60) "wounded")
      ((< health 100) "healthy")
      (t "perfect"))
@end example

@strong{Example 2: COND with Multiple Types**:
@example
(cond ((= status-code #xFF) "error")
      ((= status-code #x00) "success")
      ((= status-code #b1010) "pending")
      (t "unknown"))
@end example

@strong{Example 3: COND in Function**:
@example
(defun describe-damage (damage-amount)
  (cond ((< damage-amount 10) "scratch")
        ((< damage-amount 25) "wound")
        ((< damage-amount 50) "serious")
        (t "critical")))
@end example

@strong{Example 4: COND with Complex Tests**:
@example
(cond ((and (> level 10) (< xp 5000)) "fast-level-up")
      ((and (> level 5) (< xp 1000)) "normal-level-up")
      ((> level 1) "slow-level-up")
      (t "newbie"))
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Nested COND**:
@example
(defun game-state-machine (state event)
  (cond ((= state 'menu)
         (cond ((= event 'start) 'playing)
               ((= event 'quit) 'quit)
               (t 'menu)))
        ((= state 'playing)
         (cond ((= event 'pause) 'paused)
               ((= event 'die) 'dead)
               (t 'playing)))
        (t state)))
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
COND is more readable than nested IF expressions for complex branching logic. The compiler
optimizes COND to efficient jump tables when tests are simple comparisons on constant values.
Multiple numeric types can be mixed in conditions for flexible game state logic.

---

@subsection GOAL Form: LOOP (Iteration)

@cindex LOOP form in GOAL
@cindex loop iteration

The @code{LOOP} form implements indefinite iteration with optional break and continue controls,
useful for game loops and state machine iterations.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
(LOOP body-expr...)
@end example

@strong{Control Statements}:
@itemize
@item @code{BREAK}: Exit the loop immediately
@item @code{CONTINUE}: Jump to next iteration
@item @code{RETURN}: Exit from containing function
@end itemize

@strong{Numeric Types Supported}:
Numeric types are used in expressions within the loop body.

@strong{Example 1: Simple Infinite Loop with Break**:
@example
(loop
  (if (player-input-available?)
      (break)))

; Waits for player input
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Loop with Counter**:
@example
(let ((counter 0))
  (loop
    (print counter)
    (set! counter (+ counter 1))
    (if (>= counter 10)
        (break))))

; Prints 0-9
@end example

@strong{Example 3: Loop Skipping Iterations**:
@example
(let ((i 0))
  (loop
    (set! i (+ i 1))
    (if (= (mod i 2) 0)
        (continue))
    (print i)
    (if (>= i 9)
        (break))))

; Prints odd numbers 1-9
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Game Loop Pattern**:
@example
(defun game-main-loop ()
  (loop
    (handle-input)
    (update-game-state)
    (render-frame)
    (if (game-should-quit?)
        (break))))
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Loop with Numeric Type Expressions**:
@example
(let ((max-iterations #xFF))
  (let ((iter 0))
    (loop
      (process-entity iter)
      (set! iter (+ iter #b1))
      (if (>= iter max-iterations)
          (break)))))

; Iterates 255 times
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
LOOP is optimized by the compiler to generate tight iteration code suitable for
frame-based game loops. The compiler analyzes loop termination conditions to generate
efficient countdown loops when possible. Numeric types support efficient array indexing
within loop bodies.

---

@subsection GOAL Form: SETQ (Variable Assignment)

@cindex SETQ form in GOAL
@cindex variable assignment

The @code{SETQ} form assigns a value to a variable. Unlike LET, SETQ modifies an existing
variable or creates a new global if the variable doesn't exist locally.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
(SETQ variable-name value-expr)
@end example

@strong{Parameters}:
@itemize
@item @code{variable-name}: Name of variable to assign
@item @code{value-expr}: Expression whose value is assigned
@end itemize

@strong{Numeric Types Supported}:
Values of any numeric type can be assigned.

@strong{Example 1: Simple Assignment**:
@example
(setq player-health 100)
(setq player-health 75)  ; Updated to 75
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Assignment with Computation**:
@example
(setq current-damage (+ base-damage weapon-bonus))
(setq new-level (- current-level 1))
@end example

@strong{Example 3: Assignment with Different Numeric Types**:
@example
(setq damage-value #xFF)
(setq counter #b0000)
(setq difficulty #o5)
(setq score 1000)
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Using Assigned Value in Condition**:
@example
(setq health (- health damage))
(if (< health 0)
    (setq health 0))
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Accumulator Pattern**:
@example
(setq total 0)
(loop
  (setq total (+ total increment))
  (if (>= total #xFF)
      (break)))
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
SETQ in GOAL follows Scheme semantics: it assigns in the current scope if the variable
is local, or to a global if not. The compiler tracks variable lifetime to generate optimal
register allocation and memory access patterns. Assignment to game state variables is
often optimized to direct hardware memory writes on target platforms.

---

@subsection GOAL Form: LAMBDA (Anonymous Function)

@cindex LAMBDA form in GOAL
@cindex anonymous function

The @code{LAMBDA} form creates an anonymous function that can be passed to higher-order
functions or invoked immediately with CALL.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
(LAMBDA (param1 param2 ... paramN) body-expr)
@end example

@strong{Parameters}:
@itemize
@item @code{param-list}: Parameters for the anonymous function
@item @code{body-expr}: Expression evaluated when function is called
@end itemize

@strong{Numeric Types Supported}:
Parameters and bodies use all four numeric types.

@strong{Example 1: Simple Lambda**:
@example
(call (lambda (x) (+ x 1)) 5)

; Result: 6
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Lambda in MAP-like Operation**:
@example
(defun apply-to-all (items fn)
  (loop
    (if (empty? items)
        (break))
    (call fn (car items))
    (setq items (cdr items))))

(apply-to-all '(1 2 3) (lambda (x) (print (* x 2))))
@end example

@strong{Example 3: Lambda with Closure**:
@example
(let ((multiplier 10))
  (call (lambda (x) (* x multiplier)) 5))

; Result: 50 (lambda captures multiplier)
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Lambda with Type Conversion**:
@example
(defun apply-damage (base-damage)
  (call (lambda (bonus)
          (+ base-damage bonus))
        #x20))

; Result: base-damage + 32
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Higher-Order Function with Lambda**:
@example
(defun filter-items (items predicate)
  (cond ((empty? items) '())
        ((call predicate (car items))
         (cons (car items) (filter-items (cdr items) predicate)))
        (t (filter-items (cdr items) predicate))))

(filter-items '(1 2 3 4 5)
              (lambda (x) (> x 2)))

; Result: (3 4 5)
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
LAMBDA enables functional programming patterns useful for game event systems and AI
behavior trees. The compiler generates inline code for small lambdas and function pointers
for larger ones, balancing code size and speed. Lambdas capture their defining scope,
enabling powerful abstractions for game object callbacks and event handlers.

---

@subsection GOAL Form: QUOTE (Quotation)

@cindex QUOTE form in GOAL
@cindex quotation

The @code{QUOTE} form prevents evaluation of an expression, returning it as a literal symbol
or list structure. This is essential for representing symbolic game data.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
(QUOTE expr)
(abbreviated as 'expr)
@end example

@strong{Parameters}:
@itemize
@item @code{expr}: Any expression to be quoted (not evaluated)
@end itemize

@strong{Example 1: Quoting Symbols**:
@example
(quote player-died)
; or: 'player-died
; Returns the symbol, not evaluated
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Quoting Lists**:
@example
'(north south east west)
; Returns list of direction symbols
@end example

@strong{Example 3: Quoting in Data Structures**:
@example
(let ((directions '(north south east west)))
  (car directions))
; Result: north
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Mixing Quoted and Evaluated Data**:
@example
(let ((location-name "tavern"))
  `(location ,location-name (north tavern-hallway)))
; Result: (location "tavern" (north tavern-hallway))
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Quoted Constants in Game Data**:
@example
(let ((enemies '(goblin orc dragon)))
  (find-enemy 'orc enemies))
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
QUOTE is critical for game state machines where symbolic states and events are passed
between systems. Compiled quoting generates zero-copy symbols stored in the binary's
data section, making symbol-based game logic very efficient. Quasiquote and unquote
enable meta-programming for code-generating game systems.

---

@subsection GOAL Form: PROGN (Sequence)

@cindex PROGN form in GOAL
@cindex sequence of expressions

The @code{PROGN} form evaluates a sequence of expressions and returns the value of the
last expression. It's used when multiple side-effects are needed in a context expecting
a single expression.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
(PROGN expr1 expr2 ... exprN)
@end example

@strong{Parameters}:
@itemize
@item @code{expr-list}: One or more expressions
@end itemize

@strong{Example 1: Simple Sequence**:
@example
(progn
  (print "Starting")
  (update-game)
  (print "Done")
  42)

; Prints messages, then returns 42
@end example

@strong{Example 2: PROGN in IF Branch**:
@example
(if (> damage 50)
    (progn
      (print "Massive damage!")
      (play-sound 'explosion)
      (shake-screen))
    (print "Minor damage"))
@end example

@strong{Example 3: PROGN for Side Effects**:
@example
(defun initialize-game ()
  (progn
    (load-assets)
    (setup-entities)
    (start-music)
    42))
@end example

@strong{Example 4: PROGN with Numeric Types**:
@example
(progn
  (setq counter #x00)
  (setq max-value #xFF)
  (+ counter max-value))

; Result: 255
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Nested PROGN**:
@example
(progn
  (print "Outer")
  (progn
    (print "Inner")
    100)
  50)

; Prints "Outer", "Inner"; returns 50
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
PROGN is often implicitly used in DEFUN bodies when multiple statements are written.
The compiler optimizes PROGN to eliminate unnecessary value passing between expressions,
making multi-step game logic very efficient. PROGN enables clean sequencing of game state
updates and resource loading during startup.

---

@subsection GOAL Form: AND (Logical Conjunction)

@cindex AND form in GOAL
@cindex logical conjunction

The @code{AND} form implements short-circuit logical AND: it evaluates expressions left-to-right,
returning false if any expression is false, otherwise returning the last value.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
(AND expr1 expr2 ... exprN)
@end example

@strong{Parameters}:
@itemize
@item @code{expr-list}: One or more expressions to evaluate
@end itemize

@strong{Example 1: Simple Conjunction**:
@example
(and (> health 0)
     (< health 100)
     (= status :active))
@end example

@strong{Example 2: AND with Short-Circuit**:
@example
(and (player-exists?)
     (get-player-health)
     (> (get-player-health) 50))

; Returns false immediately if player doesn't exist
@end example

@strong{Example 3: AND with Numeric Comparisons**:
@example
(and (>= score #x100)
     (<= score #xFF)
     (= difficulty #b11))
@end example

@strong{Example 4: AND in Function**:
@example
(defun can-cast-spell? (mana-cost health-cost)
  (and (>= current-mana mana-cost)
       (> current-health health-cost)
       (spell-available?)))
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Nested AND**:
@example
(and (valid-player?)
     (and (valid-weapon?) (weapon-ready?)))
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
AND short-circuits, making it efficient for checking preconditions before expensive
operations. This is critical for game performance where checking game state validity
might be expensive. The compiler generates code that jumps directly to the false case
without evaluating remaining conditions.

---

@subsection GOAL Form: OR (Logical Disjunction)

@cindex OR form in GOAL
@cindex logical disjunction

The @code{OR} form implements short-circuit logical OR: it evaluates expressions left-to-right,
returning true (the first true value) if any expression is true, otherwise returning false.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
(OR expr1 expr2 ... exprN)
@end example

@strong{Parameters}:
@itemize
@item @code{expr-list}: One or more expressions to evaluate
@end itemize

@strong{Example 1: Simple Disjunction**:
@example
(or (= status 'dead)
    (= status 'dying)
    (= status 'defeated))
@end example

@strong{Example 2: OR with Short-Circuit**:
@example
(or (player-in-safe-zone?)
    (no-enemies-nearby?)
    (can-escape?))

; Returns true at first true condition
@end example

@strong{Example 3: OR with Numeric Types**:
@example
(or (= code #xFF)
    (= code #b1111)
    (= code #o377))
@end example

@strong{Example 4: OR in Combat Logic**:
@example
(defun should-flee? ()
  (or (< health 20)
      (> enemy-count 3)
      (= ammo 0)))
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Combining AND and OR**:
@example
(and (player-alive?)
     (or (player-has-key?)
         (player-has-spell?)
         (player-can-break-door?)))
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
OR is commonly used in game AI decision trees where multiple alternative actions are
considered. Short-circuit evaluation is critical for performance; expensive AI checks
are avoided when an earlier condition already determines the outcome. The compiler
generates efficient fallthrough jump code for OR expressions.

---

@subsection GOAL Form: NOT (Logical Negation)

@cindex NOT form in GOAL
@cindex logical negation

The @code{NOT} form inverts the boolean value of an expression, returning true for false
values and false for true values.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
(NOT expr)
@end example

@strong{Parameters}:
@itemize
@item @code{expr}: Expression to negate
@end itemize

@strong{Example 1: Simple Negation**:
@example
(not (player-alive?))  ; True if player is dead
@end example

@strong{Example 2: NOT in Conditional**:
@example
(if (not (inventory-full?))
    (pickup-item item))
@end example

@strong{Example 3: NOT with Comparison**:
@example
(not (= current-time midnight))
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Double Negation**:
@example
(not (not (player-has-weapon?)))  ; Same as (player-has-weapon?)
@end example

@strong{Example 5: NOT in Filtering**:
@example
(defun filter-inactive-entities (entities)
  (cond ((empty? entities) '())
        ((not (entity-active? (car entities)))
         (filter-inactive-entities (cdr entities)))
        (t (cons (car entities)
                 (filter-inactive-entities (cdr entities))))))
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
NOT is often used to make game logic more readable ("if NOT paused" vs "if paused
is false"). The compiler typically implements NOT as a simple conditional branch,
generating highly efficient code on all target architectures.

---

@node ZIL Language Forms
@section ZIL Language Forms

ZIL (Zork Implementation Language) is an interactive fiction language designed for creating
text adventure games. This section documents 10+ primary ZIL forms with complete syntax,
numeric types, real examples, and domain-specific features.

@subsection ZIL Form: OBJECT (Object Definition)

@cindex OBJECT form in ZIL
@cindex object definition in interactive fiction

The @code{OBJECT} form defines a game object with properties, descriptions, and behaviors.
Objects are the fundamental unit of the ZIL game world.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
<OBJECT object-name
  (DESCRIPTION "string")
  (ADJECTIVES word1 word2 ...)
  (NOUNS noun1 noun2 ...)
  (SIZE number)
  (WEIGHT number)
  (VALUE number)
  (FLAGS flag1 flag2 ...>
@end example

@strong{Numeric Types Supported}:
@itemize
@item @code{BINARY}: Binary literals for bit flags (0B1010, 0B11110000)
@item @code{DECIMAL}: Integer properties (size, weight, value)
@item @code{DISPLAY}: Display numbers for readable game values
@end itemize

@strong{Example 1: Simple Object**:
@example
<OBJECT SWORD
  (DESCRIPTION "a rusty sword")
  (ADJECTIVES "rusty" "iron")
  (NOUNS "sword")
  (SIZE 10)
  (WEIGHT 50)
  (VALUE 25)>
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Object with FLAGS (Binary Type)**:
@example
<OBJECT TORCH
  (DESCRIPTION "a burning torch")
  (NOUNS "torch" "light")
  (SIZE 5)
  (WEIGHT 20)
  (FLAGS 0B1010)  ; Binary: can-take, glows
  (VALUE 15)>
@end example

@strong{Example 3: Object with DISPLAY Type Numbers**:
@example
<OBJECT TREASURE-CHEST
  (DESCRIPTION "a large wooden chest")
  (NOUNS "chest" "box")
  (SIZE 100)
  (WEIGHT 500)
  (VALUE 1000)  ; Display as is
  (CAPACITY 50)>
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Container Object**:
@example
<OBJECT BACKPACK
  (DESCRIPTION "a worn leather backpack")
  (NOUNS "backpack" "pack" "bag")
  (SIZE 30)
  (WEIGHT 10)
  (VALUE 5)
  (CAPACITY 100)
  (HAS ROPE KEY POTION)>
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Door Object with Complex Properties**:
@example
<OBJECT WOODEN-DOOR
  (DESCRIPTION "a heavy wooden door")
  (ADJECTIVES "wooden" "heavy")
  (NOUNS "door" "entrance")
  (SIZE 200)
  (WEIGHT 0)  ; Immovable
  (VALUE 0)
  (FLAGS 0B1111)  ; Multiple flags
  (PROPERTY strength 50)>
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
Objects in ZIL can contain other objects, have custom properties, and trigger events when
examined or taken. The compiler optimizes object tables for memory-efficient game worlds
suitable for 8-bit systems. Object state (flags, properties) can be modified at runtime
to track game progress.

---

@subsection ZIL Form: ROOM (Room Definition)

@cindex ROOM form in ZIL
@cindex room definition

The @code{ROOM} form defines a location in the game world with descriptions, exits,
and objects.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
<ROOM room-name
  (DESCRIPTION "long description")
  (SHORT "short name")
  (NORTH to-room)
  (SOUTH to-room)
  (EAST to-room)
  (WEST to-room)
  (UP to-room)
  (DOWN to-room)
  (OBJECTS object1 object2 ...)>
@end example

@strong{Numeric Types Supported}:
Properties associated with rooms use DECIMAL and DISPLAY types.

@strong{Example 1: Simple Room**:
@example
<ROOM TAVERN
  (DESCRIPTION "You are in a dimly lit tavern.")
  (SHORT "Tavern")
  (NORTH HALL)
  (OBJECTS BARTENDER BEER-KEG)>
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Room with All Exits**:
@example
<ROOM CROSSROADS
  (SHORT "Crossroads")
  (DESCRIPTION "A five-way crossroads.")
  (NORTH TEMPLE)
  (SOUTH FOREST)
  (EAST CASTLE)
  (WEST VILLAGE)
  (UP MOUNTAIN)
  (DOWN DUNGEON)>
@end example

@strong{Example 3: Room with Objects and Properties**:
@example
<ROOM BEDROOM
  (SHORT "Bedroom")
  (DESCRIPTION "A comfortable bedroom with furniture.")
  (SOUTH HALLWAY)
  (OBJECTS BED WARDROBE CHEST)
  (PROPERTY danger-level 0)
  (PROPERTY illumination 50)>
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Dark Room**:
@example
<ROOM DUNGEON-CELL
  (SHORT "Dark Cell")
  (DESCRIPTION "It is pitch black.")
  (FLAGS 0B0001)  ; Dark room flag
  (EAST DUNGEON-CORRIDOR)
  (OBJECTS SKELETON CHAINS)>
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Dangerous Room**:
@example
<ROOM DRAGON-LAIR
  (SHORT "Dragon's Lair")
  (DESCRIPTION "A vast cavern filled with gold and treasure...")
  (SOUTH MOUNTAINS)
  (OBJECTS DRAGON GOLD-PILE)
  (PROPERTY danger-level 100)
  (PROPERTY temperature 500)>
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
Rooms form the interconnected graph structure of an adventure game world. The compiler
generates efficient exit tables and object inventories. Room descriptions can be conditional
and change based on game state. Room properties enable environmental effects like lighting,
temperature, and danger levels.

---

@subsection ZIL Form: VERB (Verb Definition)

@cindex VERB form in ZIL
@cindex verb definition

The @code{VERB} form defines an action the player can take, specifying syntax patterns and
responses.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
<VERB verb-name
  (SYNTAX "pattern1")
  (SYNTAX "pattern2")
  ...
  (HANDLER routine-name)>
@end example

@strong{Example 1: Simple Verb**:
@example
<VERB LOOK
  (SYNTAX "LOOK")
  (SYNTAX "LOOK AT object")
  (HANDLER V-LOOK)>
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Multi-pattern Verb**:
@example
<VERB TAKE
  (SYNTAX "TAKE object")
  (SYNTAX "GET object")
  (SYNTAX "GRAB object")
  (HANDLER V-TAKE)>
@end example

@strong{Example 3: Directional Verb**:
@example
<VERB GO
  (SYNTAX "GO direction")
  (SYNTAX "direction")
  (SYNTAX "MOVE direction")
  (HANDLER V-GO)>
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Combat Verb**:
@example
<VERB ATTACK
  (SYNTAX "ATTACK enemy")
  (SYNTAX "FIGHT enemy")
  (SYNTAX "KILL enemy")
  (HANDLER V-ATTACK)>
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Magic Verb**:
@example
<VERB CAST
  (SYNTAX "CAST spell")
  (SYNTAX "CAST spell AT target")
  (HANDLER V-CAST)>
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
VERBs provide the player's primary interface to the game. The parser matches player input
against verb syntax patterns and dispatches to handlers. Custom verbs enable game-specific
commands. Verb handlers have access to parsed arguments, allowing context-sensitive responses.

---

@subsection ZIL Form: DEFINE (Subroutine Definition)

@cindex DEFINE form in ZIL
@cindex subroutine definition

The @code{DEFINE} form defines a subroutine (routine) that implements game logic,
verb handlers, and support functions.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
<DEFINE routine-name (optional-args)
  (LOCAL var1 var2 ...)
  statement1
  statement2
  ...
  statementN>
@end example

@strong{Numeric Types Supported**:
Routines work with BINARY, DECIMAL, and DISPLAY numeric types.

@strong{Example 1: Simple Routine**:
@example
<DEFINE V-LOOK ()
  (PRINT "You look around.")
  (PRINT-OBJECTS)>
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Routine with Parameters**:
@example
<DEFINE V-TAKE (OBJECT)
  (LOCAL CARRY-WEIGHT)
  (SET CARRY-WEIGHT (+ CURRENT-WEIGHT (GETPROP OBJECT 'WEIGHT)))
  (IF (> CARRY-WEIGHT 100)
      (PRINT "Too heavy!")
      (PROGN
        (MOVE OBJECT PLAYER)
        (PRINT "Taken."))))
@end example

@strong{Example 3: Routine with Local Variables**:
@example
<DEFINE CALCULATE-DAMAGE (WEAPON)
  (LOCAL BASE-DAMAGE MODIFIER TOTAL)
  (SET BASE-DAMAGE (GETPROP WEAPON 'DAMAGE))
  (SET MODIFIER (RANDOM 10))
  (SET TOTAL (+ BASE-DAMAGE MODIFIER))
  TOTAL>
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Recursive Routine**:
@example
<DEFINE COUNT-OBJECTS (OBJECT COUNT)
  (LOCAL NEXT-OBJECT)
  (SET NEXT-OBJECT (SIBLING OBJECT))
  (IF NEXT-OBJECT
      (COUNT-OBJECTS NEXT-OBJECT (+ COUNT 1))
      COUNT)>
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Routine with Binary Numeric Types**:
@example
<DEFINE CHECK-FLAGS (FLAGS MASK)
  (LOCAL RESULT)
  (SET RESULT (BAND FLAGS MASK))
  (IF (EQUAL RESULT 0B1010)
      (PRINT "Match!")
      (PRINT "No match."))>
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
Routines are the primary unit of game logic in ZIL. They can be nested (defining local
functions) and support tail recursion optimization. Local variables are scoped to the
routine. Routines return values, enabling compositional game logic. The compiler generates
efficient procedure call conventions for target CPUs.

---

@subsection ZIL Form: SET (Local Variable Assignment)

@cindex SET form in ZIL

The @code{SET} form assigns a value to a local variable within a routine.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
(SET variable-name value-expr)
@end example

@strong{Example 1: Simple Assignment**:
@example
(SET HEALTH 100)
(SET DAMAGE (+ BASE-DAMAGE BONUS))
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Assignment with Different Types**:
@example
(SET FLAGS 0B1010)
(SET COUNT 42)
(SET MAX-VALUE 255)
@end example

@strong{Example 3: Assignment in Conditional**:
@example
(IF (> DAMAGE 50)
    (SET CRITICAL-HIT 1)
    (SET CRITICAL-HIT 0))
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Assignment in Loop**:
@example
(LOCAL COUNTER)
(SET COUNTER 0)
(LOOP
  (PRINT COUNTER)
  (SET COUNTER (+ COUNTER 1))
  (IF (>= COUNTER 10) (BREAK)))
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Assignment in Calculation**:
@example
(SET TOTAL-DAMAGE (+ WEAPON-DAMAGE SPELL-DAMAGE))
(SET REMAINING-HEALTH (- HEALTH TOTAL-DAMAGE))
(IF (< REMAINING-HEALTH 0)
    (SET REMAINING-HEALTH 0))
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
SET is critical for game state management. The compiler tracks variable assignments to
optimize local variable allocation. Assignments to game state properties are often
translated to direct memory writes on target platforms.

---

@subsection ZIL Form: GET and PUT (Property Access)

@cindex GET form in ZIL
@cindex PUT form in ZIL
@cindex property access

The @code{GET} form retrieves an object property, and @code{PUT} sets it.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
(GET object-name property-name)
(PUT object-name property-name value)
@end example

@strong{Example 1: Getting Properties**:
@example
(GET SWORD 'WEIGHT)      ; Returns weight
(GET PLAYER 'HEALTH)     ; Returns player health
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Setting Properties**:
@example
(PUT PLAYER 'HEALTH 100)
(PUT SWORD 'CONDITION 80)
@end example

@strong{Example 3: Modifying Properties in Calculation**:
@example
(LOCAL CURRENT-HP)
(SET CURRENT-HP (GET PLAYER 'HEALTH))
(SET CURRENT-HP (- CURRENT-HP 25))
(PUT PLAYER 'HEALTH CURRENT-HP)
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Property-based Conditionals**:
@example
(IF (> (GET WEAPON 'DAMAGE) 50)
    (PRINT "Powerful weapon!")
    (PRINT "Weak weapon."))
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Looping Through Object Properties**:
@example
(LOCAL ARMOR-VALUE)
(LOOP
  (IF (EMPTY? INVENTORY)
      (BREAK))
  (SET ARMOR-VALUE (GET (CAR INVENTORY) 'ARMOR))
  (ADD-TO-TOTAL ARMOR-VALUE)
  (SET INVENTORY (CDR INVENTORY)))
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
Properties are the primary way to attach custom data to objects. The compiler generates
efficient property lookup tables. Built-in properties (WEIGHT, VALUE, SIZE) are optimized
for performance. Custom properties enable extensible game objects.

---

@subsection ZIL Form: IF (Conditional)

@cindex IF form in ZIL

The @code{IF} form implements conditional logic similar to GOAL but with ZIL's
specific syntax.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
<IF test-expr
    then-expr
    [ELSE else-expr]>
@end example

@strong{Example 1: Simple Conditional**:
@example
<IF (> PLAYER-HEALTH 0)
    (PRINT "You are alive.")
    ELSE
    (PRINT "You are dead.")>
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Nested Conditionals**:
@example
<IF (PLAYER-HAS-WEAPON?)
    <IF (WEAPON-LOADED?) 
        (FIRE)
        ELSE
        (RELOAD)>
    ELSE
    (PRINT "You need a weapon!")>
@end example

@strong{Example 3: Complex Conditions**:
@example
<IF (AND (> LEVEL 5) (< XP 1000) (= STATUS 'ACTIVE))
    (LEVEL-UP)
    ELSE
    (PRINT "Not ready yet.")>
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Conditionals with Numeric Types**:
@example
<IF (EQUAL FLAGS 0B1010)
    (SET RESULT 255)
    ELSE
    (SET RESULT 0)>
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Conditional in Object Method**:
@example
<DEFINE ATTACK-ENEMY (ENEMY)
  <IF (> ENEMY-HEALTH 0)
      (PROGN
        (SET DAMAGE (RANDOM 20))
        (PUT ENEMY 'HEALTH (- (GET ENEMY 'HEALTH) DAMAGE))
        (PRINT "Hit!"))
      ELSE
      (PRINT "Enemy already dead.")>>
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
IF is used extensively in interactive fiction for branching narratives and game logic.
The compiler optimizes IF for tight conditional code generation. Nested IFs create
complex decision trees essential for non-linear game flow.

---

@subsection ZIL Form: COND (Multi-Branch Conditional)

@cindex COND form in ZIL

The @code{COND} form provides multi-branch conditional logic, similar to GOAL's COND.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
<COND (test1 expr1)
      (test2 expr2)
      ...
      (ELSE default-expr)>
@end example

@strong{Example 1: Simple COND**:
@example
<COND ((= GAME-STATE 'PLAYING)
       (UPDATE-GAME))
      ((= GAME-STATE 'PAUSED)
       (SHOW-PAUSE-MENU))
      ((= GAME-STATE 'QUIT)
       (EXIT))
      (ELSE
       (PRINT "Unknown state"))>
@end example

@strong{Example 2: COND with Object Types**:
@example
<COND ((EQUAL OBJECT-TYPE 'WEAPON)
       (EQUIP-WEAPON OBJECT))
      ((EQUAL OBJECT-TYPE 'POTION)
       (DRINK-POTION OBJECT))
      ((EQUAL OBJECT-TYPE 'KEY)
       (ADD-TO-KEYRING OBJECT))
      (ELSE
       (PRINT "Don't know what to do."))>
@end example

@strong{Example 3: COND with Numeric Comparisons**:
@example
<COND ((< HEALTH 20)
       (PRINT "Critical health!"))
      ((< HEALTH 50)
       (PRINT "Wounded"))
      ((< HEALTH 100)
       (PRINT "Healthy"))
      (ELSE
       (PRINT "Perfect health"))>
@end example

@strong{Example 4: COND in Spell Selection**:
@example
<COND ((AND (> MANA 50) (NOT FATIGUED?))
       (CAST-POWERFUL-SPELL))
      ((> MANA 20)
       (CAST-NORMAL-SPELL))
      ((> MANA 5)
       (CAST-WEAK-SPELL))
      (ELSE
       (PRINT "Not enough mana"))>
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Nested COND**:
@example
<COND ((PLAYER-IN-COMBAT?)
       <COND ((HAVE-WEAPON?)
              (ATTACK))
             ((HAVE-SPELL?)
              (CAST-SPELL))
             (ELSE
              (RUN-AWAY))))
      ((PLAYER-IN-CONVERSATION?)
       (HANDLE-DIALOGUE))
      (ELSE
       (PRINT "Idle"))>
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
COND is preferred over nested IF for complex game state machines. The compiler can optimize
COND branches to jump tables when test conditions are simple comparisons. Multi-branch
conditionals are essential for adventure game parsing where many verb variations must be
handled.

---

@subsection ZIL Form: LOOP (Iteration)

@cindex LOOP form in ZIL

The @code{LOOP} form implements indefinite iteration with break and continue controls.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
<LOOP
  statement1
  statement2
  ...>
@end example

@strong{Example 1: Simple Loop**:
@example
<LOOP
  (PRINT "Game loop iteration")
  (UPDATE-ENTITIES)
  (RENDER-FRAME)>
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Loop with Counter and Break**:
@example
(LOCAL I)
(SET I 0)
<LOOP
  (PRINT I)
  (SET I (+ I 1))
  (IF (>= I 10) (BREAK))>
@end example

@strong{Example 3: Loop Processing Objects**:
@example
(LOCAL CURRENT)
(SET CURRENT INVENTORY)
<LOOP
  (IF (EMPTY? CURRENT) (BREAK))
  (PROCESS-ITEM (CAR CURRENT))
  (SET CURRENT (CDR CURRENT))>
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Game Main Loop**:
@example
<LOOP
  (HANDLE-INPUT)
  (UPDATE-GAME-STATE)
  (UPDATE-ENEMIES)
  (UPDATE-EFFECTS)
  (RENDER)
  (IF (QUIT-REQUESTED?) (BREAK))>
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Loop with Continue**:
@example
(LOCAL I)
(SET I 0)
<LOOP
  (SET I (+ I 1))
  (IF (EQUAL (MOD I 2) 0) (CONTINUE))
  (PRINT I)
  (IF (>= I 9) (BREAK))>
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
LOOP implements the frame-based game loop essential for real-time adventure games.
The compiler generates tight iteration code suitable for 8-bit targets. Tail recursion
can replace LOOP for functional-style game logic. LOOP with break provides a clean
exit mechanism from game loops.

---

@node Fountain Language Directives
@section Fountain Language Directives

Fountain is a screenplay language designed for game dialogue, cutscenes, and narrative scripts.
This section documents 8+ primary Fountain directives with complete syntax, numeric types,
real examples, and domain-specific features.

@subsection Fountain Directive: Scene Headings

@cindex Fountain scene headings
@cindex INT, EXT, BLOB

Fountain scene headings (sluglines) introduce new scenes with location and time information.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
INT LOCATION - DESCRIPTION
EXT LOCATION - DESCRIPTION
BLOB TITLE (SPECIAL)
@end example

@strong{Numeric Types Supported}:
Scene numbers use DECIMAL and DISPLAY types.

@strong{Example 1: Interior Scene**:
@example
INT TAVERN - DIMLY LIT, AFTERNOON
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Exterior Scene**:
@example
EXT FOREST CLEARING - MORNING, MISTY
@end example

@strong{Example 3: BLOB Title Card**:
@example
BLOB CHAPTER 1 - THE BEGINNING
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Night Scene**:
@example
INT CASTLE CHAMBER - NIGHT
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Scene with Transition**:
@example
EXT MOUNTAIN PEAK - SUNSET
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
Scene headings establish the context for dialogue and action sequences. Time of day
information can affect game logic (spawning specific NPCs, changing lighting). Scene
descriptions can trigger environmental effects.

---

@subsection Fountain Directive: Character Entries

@cindex Fountain character entries

Character entries describe when characters enter scenes, with optional poses and directions.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
Enter CHARACTER [Looks ADJ] [Faces DIRECTION]
@end example

@strong{Example 1: Simple Entry**:
@example
Enter Hero
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Entry with Pose**:
@example
Enter Knight Looks brave Faces north
@end example

@strong{Example 3: Multiple Characters**:
@example
Enter Hero
Enter Companion Looks worried
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Entry at Location**:
@example
Enter Villain Looks menacing Faces Hero
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Staged Entries**:
@example
Enter Guard
Enter Guard
Enter Captain Looks commanding
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
Character entries define NPC appearance states and facing directions for 3D game worlds.
Multiple entries of the same character enable crowd scenes. Entry poses can trigger
animation sequences.

---

@subsection Fountain Directive: Action

@cindex Fountain action directive

Action lines describe what happens in a scene without dialogue.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
ACTION description text
@end example

@strong{Example 1: Simple Action**:
@example
ACTION Hero draws sword and points it at the dragon.
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Dramatic Action**:
@example
ACTION The ground shakes. A massive cave opens in the earth.
@end example

@strong{Example 3: Action with Sound Effects**:
@example
ACTION Door SLAMS open. Wind howls through the chamber.
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Environmental Action**:
@example
ACTION Lightning flashes. Thunder echoes across the mountains.
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Character Action**:
@example
ACTION Villain laughs menacingly and raises staff.
ACTION Magical flames erupt from the staff.
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
Action lines trigger animations and special effects in interactive fiction. Sound
effect descriptions can be parsed and played. Timing of actions can be controlled
to synchronize with dialogue.

---

@subsection Fountain Directive: Character Names and Dialogue

@cindex Fountain dialogue
@cindex character dialogue

Character dialogue is introduced with the character name, followed by the spoken text.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
CHARACTER-NAME
dialogue text
@end example

@strong{Example 1: Simple Dialogue**:
@example
HERO
I must stop the evil wizard!
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Extended Dialogue**:
@example
WIZARD
You think you can defeat me? I have studied the ancient magics
for centuries. Your puny sword cannot touch me!
@end example

@strong{Example 3: Question Dialogue**:
@example
COMPANION
Where are we going now?
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Emotional Dialogue**:
@example
HERO
No! She cannot be dead. There must be a way...
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Multiple Characters**:
@example
HERO
Where is the treasure?

GUIDE
Over that ridge, if we survive the mountain trolls.

HERO
Trolls? How many?

GUIDE
More than we have arrows for.
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
Dialogue is parsed to extract character speech for subtitles and voice synthesis.
Emotional context can be inferred to adjust voice tone or animation. Question-answer
patterns support branching dialogue trees.

---

@subsection Fountain Directive: Parentheticals

@cindex Fountain parentheticals

Parentheticals provide stage directions for dialogue delivery, appearing below character
names and describing how the line should be delivered.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
(parenthetical direction)
@end example

@strong{Example 1: Simple Parenthetical**:
@example
HERO
(angrily)
You will not get away with this!
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Action Parenthetical**:
@example
WIZARD
(raises staff, magical energy crackling)
Feel the power of the arcane!
@end example

@strong{Example 3: Emotional Parenthetical**:
@example
COMPANION
(sadly)
I never thought it would end like this.
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Directed Parenthetical**:
@example
HERO
(to the Wizard)
You know this is wrong!
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Timing Parenthetical**:
@example
GUARD
(after a long pause)
The dungeons are that way, stranger.
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
Parentheticals control dialogue delivery tone and trigger animations. They are often
mapped to voice effect codes (angry, whisper, excited) for voice synthesis. Timing
parentheticals control dialogue pacing in cutscenes.

---

@subsection Fountain Directive: Transitions

@cindex Fountain transitions

Transitions specify how scenes change, providing visual effects between locations.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
FADE TO BLACK
CUT TO
DISSOLVE TO
WIPE
@end example

@strong{Example 1: Fade Transition**:
@example
FADE TO BLACK
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Cut Transition**:
@example
CUT TO
@end example

@strong{Example 3: Dissolve Transition**:
@example
DISSOLVE TO
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Wipe Transition**:
@example
WIPE
@end example

@strong{Example 5: With Direction**:
@example
FADE TO ORANGE
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
Transitions control screen effects and timing between scenes. FADE and DISSOLVE can
specify colors for specialized effects. Transitions control pacing in cutscenes and
dialogue-heavy sequences.

---

@subsection Fountain Directive: Page Breaks

@cindex Fountain page breaks

Page breaks control where screenplay pages end, affecting printing and formatting.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
PAGE BREAK
---
@end example

@strong{Example 1: Explicit Page Break**:
@example
PAGE BREAK
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Triple Dash Page Break**:
@example
---
@end example

@strong{Example 3: Page Break in Script**:
@example
Hero enters the throne room.

HERO
This ends now!

---

INT DUNGEON BELOW - MOMENTS LATER
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Multiple Page Breaks**:
@example
---

---

INT EPILOGUE - ONE YEAR LATER
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Page Break Control**:
@example
The spell activates with tremendous power.

---

EXT WORLD - TRANSFORMED
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
Page breaks control document layout and cutscene scene divisions. Multiple page breaks
can create dramatic pauses in the narrative. Page breaks separate acts and chapters
in interactive fiction.

---

@subsection Fountain Directive: Notes

@cindex Fountain notes

Notes provide documentation and metadata without appearing in the rendered output.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
[[ note text ]]
@end example

@strong{Example 1: Simple Note**:
@example
[[ This is the opening scene ]]
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Stage Direction Note**:
@example
[[ TODO: Add explosion sound effect here ]]
@end example

@strong{Example 3: Timing Note**:
@example
[[ This dialogue should take about 30 seconds ]]
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Effect Note**:
@example
[[ Trigger dramatic lighting shift when hero draws sword ]]
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Implementation Note**:
@example
[[ This is a branching point - player chooses left or right ]]
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
Notes are critical for game scripting, documenting branch points, trigger conditions,
and effect timing. Notes can be extracted to generate game logic annotations. Notes
enable non-programmers to write interactive fiction with technical annotations.

---

@node Burgermistress Language Statements
@section Burgermistress Language Statements

Burgermistress is a hybrid language combining BASIC-like syntax with Prolog-style
logic programming. This section documents 12+ primary Burgermistress statements
with complete syntax, numeric types, real examples, and domain-specific features.

@subsection Burgermistress Statement: Variable Declaration

@cindex Burgermistress variable declaration

Variable declarations introduce new variables with optional initialization.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
DIM VariableName [AS TYPE]
DIM VariableName = InitialValue
@end example

@strong{Numeric Types Supported}:
@itemize
@item @code{BINARY}: Binary literals (0B1010, 0B11110000)
@item @code{DECIMAL}: Integer values (0, 42, 255)
@item @code{DISPLAY}: Display numbers for game values
@end itemize

@strong{Example 1: Simple Declaration**:
@example
DIM PlayerHealth
PlayerHealth = 100
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Declaration with Type**:
@example
DIM Damage AS INTEGER
DIM EnemyName AS STRING
@end example

@strong{Example 3: Declaration with Initialization**:
@example
DIM MaxHealth = 100
DIM Armor = 25
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Multiple Declarations**:
@example
DIM Experience = 0
DIM Level = 1
DIM Gold = 0
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Declarations with Different Types**:
@example
DIM Flags = 0B1010
DIM Count = 0
DIM MaxValue = 255
DIM Description = "Player"
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
Burgermistress declarations support dynamic typing with optional type annotations. Numeric
types are transparently converted during compilation. Initialization expressions are evaluated
at declaration time, enabling complex game state setup.

