@subsection Burgermistress Statement: SUBROUTINE Definition

@cindex Burgermistress SUBROUTINE

SUBROUTINE statements define named procedures.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
SUB SubroutineName(param1, param2, ...)
  statements
END SUB
@end example

@strong{Example 1: Simple Subroutine**:
@example
SUB PrintGreeting()
  Print "Hello!"
END SUB
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Subroutine with Parameters**:
@example
SUB TakeDamage(Amount)
  Health = Health - Amount
  IF Health < 0 THEN
    Health = 0
  END IF
END SUB
@end example

@strong{Example 3: Subroutine with Multiple Parameters**:
@example
SUB CalculateDamage(Base, Modifier, CriticalChance)
  Total = Base + Modifier
  IF Random() < CriticalChance THEN
    Total = Total * 2
  END IF
  RETURN Total
END SUB
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Recursive Subroutine**:
@example
SUB Factorial(N)
  IF N <= 1 THEN
    RETURN 1
  ELSE
    RETURN N * Factorial(N - 1)
  END IF
END SUB
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Subroutine with Local Variables**:
@example
SUB ProcessEntity(Entity)
  LOCAL Health, MaxHealth, DamageAmount
  Health = GetHealth(Entity)
  MaxHealth = GetMaxHealth(Entity)
  DamageAmount = CalculateIncomingDamage()
  SetHealth(Entity, Health - DamageAmount)
END SUB
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
Burgermistress subroutines support arbitrary parameter counts and recursive calls. Local
variables are scoped to the subroutine. The compiler generates efficient procedure call
conventions. Return statements are implicit (last expression value) or explicit with RETURN.

