@subsection Burgermistress Statement: Return

@cindex Burgermistress RETURN

The RETURN statement exits a subroutine, optionally returning a value.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
RETURN [value]
@end example

@strong{Example 1: Simple Return**:
@example
SUB Test()
  IF Error THEN
    RETURN
  END IF
END SUB
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Return with Value**:
@example
SUB GetHealth()
  RETURN Health
END SUB
@end example

@strong{Example 3: Return in Conditional**:
@example
SUB ValidatePlayer(Player)
  IF Player.Health <= 0 THEN
    RETURN 0
  ELSE
    RETURN 1
  END IF
END SUB
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Multiple Returns**:
@example
SUB CalculateDamage(Weapon, Armor)
  IF Weapon = NULL THEN
    RETURN 0
  END IF
  IF Armor > Weapon.Power THEN
    RETURN 0
  ELSE
    RETURN Weapon.Power - Armor
  END IF
END SUB
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Return from Recursive Function**:
@example
SUB Sum(N)
  IF N <= 0 THEN
    RETURN 0
  ELSE
    RETURN N + Sum(N - 1)
  END IF
END SUB
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
RETURN enables early exit from subroutines for error handling. Return values are essential
for functional programming patterns. The compiler optimizes RETURN to efficient procedure
exit code, often single instructions on target platforms.

