@subsection Burgermistress Statement: CONTINUE

@cindex Burgermistress CONTINUE

The CONTINUE statement skips to the next loop iteration.

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
CONTINUE [DO | FOR]
@end example

@strong{Example 1: Simple Continue**:
@example
FOR I = 0 TO 10
  IF I MOD 2 = 0 THEN
    CONTINUE
  END IF
  Print I
NEXT
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Continue in DO Loop**:
@example
DO
  IF SkipFrame THEN
    CONTINUE DO
  END IF
  ProcessFrame()
LOOP
@end example

@strong{Example 3: Continue Skipping Initialization**:
@example
FOR I = 0 TO 100
  IF Entity(I).Dead THEN
    CONTINUE FOR
  END IF
  UpdateEntity(I)
NEXT I
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Continue in Nested Loop**:
@example
FOR X = 0 TO 9
  FOR Y = 0 TO 9
    IF Grid(X, Y) = EMPTY THEN
      CONTINUE FOR
    END IF
    ProcessCell(X, Y)
  NEXT Y
NEXT X
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Filtering Loop**:
@example
FOR I = 0 TO ItemCount
  IF Item(I).Type = JUNK THEN
    CONTINUE FOR
  END IF
  ProcessItem(Item(I))
NEXT I
@end example

@strong{Domain-Specific Features}:
CONTINUE enables clean filtering logic in loops. Specifying DO or FOR clarifies which
loop level to continue (useful in nested loops). The compiler optimizes CONTINUE to
direct jump-to-next-iteration code.

